1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:06,864
It is a quiet Tuesday in the data centre of the bank, and the platform team sets up heeyID for the first time.

2
00:00:06,914 --> 00:00:11,736
One line is missing from the configuration: the lifetime of a one-time code.

3
00:00:11,786 --> 00:00:17,096
What should the platform do: guess a number, or stop?

4
00:00:17,146 --> 00:00:22,544
In Lesson 5, each app got its own required level, and each token its own lifetime.

5
00:00:22,594 --> 00:00:27,872
This lesson shows who sets values like these, where they live, and how they change.

6
00:00:27,922 --> 00:00:29,456
Many systems would guess.

7
00:00:29,506 --> 00:00:34,160
Somewhere in the code there is a constant, and the constant wins, quietly.

8
00:00:34,210 --> 00:00:37,376
In heeyID, the code has no default value at all.

9
00:00:37,426 --> 00:00:41,528
I think a default hidden in the code is a decision that nobody signed.

10
00:00:41,578 --> 00:00:46,352
Nobody sees it, nobody approves it, and nobody can say when it changed.

11
00:00:46,402 --> 00:00:51,944
So the defaults live in one place: the OpenTofu configuration of the installation.

12
00:00:51,994 --> 00:00:55,976
OpenTofu is the tool that describes the installation as code.

13
00:00:56,026 --> 00:01:04,832
Its catalogue holds a value for each policy in the requirements, and the default texts for consent, terms and notifications, in four languages.

14
00:01:04,882 --> 00:01:09,776
At setup, the platform reads this catalogue and writes it as version zero.

15
00:01:09,826 --> 00:01:16,424
The proposer of version zero is the product, and each row has a mark that says: this is still the default.

16
00:01:16,474 --> 00:01:21,632
If one value is missing, the setup stops, with a clear error that names the value.

17
00:01:21,682 --> 00:01:29,960
So on that Tuesday, the platform team sees the missing code lifetime in the first minute, not in a complaint from a customer a month later.

18
00:01:30,010 --> 00:01:32,600
There is a second gate before the first customer.

19
00:01:32,650 --> 00:01:38,576
The installation does not start its service until the bank has at least one active app with a required level.

20
00:01:38,626 --> 00:01:43,400
Until then, every journey answers: the service is not available.

21
00:01:43,450 --> 00:01:46,352
Two kinds of values sit in that configuration.

22
00:01:46,402 --> 00:01:52,328
Policies are settings of the bank: code lifetimes, attempt limits and waiting times.

23
00:01:52,378 --> 00:02:02,672
Installation keys belong to one zone: the MyID host, the zone itself, whether test customers are allowed, the breaker values and the access token lifetime.

24
00:02:02,722 --> 00:02:05,840
The bank can change a policy in the administrator panel.

25
00:02:05,890 --> 00:02:08,312
Nobody can change an installation key there.

26
00:02:08,362 --> 00:02:10,016
Now a policy changes.

27
00:02:10,066 --> 00:02:16,664
Months later, Malika, a security administrator of the bank, wants a shorter lifetime for one-time codes.

28
00:02:16,714 --> 00:02:19,472
She opens the panel, and makes a proposal.

29
00:02:19,522 --> 00:02:23,600
The proposal shows the current value, the new value and her reason.

30
00:02:23,650 --> 00:02:25,568
The value does not change yet.

31
00:02:25,618 --> 00:02:31,784
A second security administrator must approve it, and Malika cannot approve her own proposal.

32
00:02:31,834 --> 00:02:37,520
This is maker-checker: one person makes a change, and a different person with the same role checks it.

33
00:02:37,570 --> 00:02:44,408
When her colleague approves, the policy gets a new version, with the proposer, the approver, the times and the reason.

34
00:02:44,458 --> 00:02:49,088
If the colleague rejects it, the rejected proposal stays, with its reason.

35
00:02:49,138 --> 00:02:55,160
And if a new value turns out wrong, a staff user with that role can propose an earlier version again.

36
00:02:55,210 --> 00:03:00,560
Texts follow the same path, with the Compliance role in place of the security administrator.

37
00:03:00,610 --> 00:03:03,728
The platform also checks each value itself.

38
00:03:03,778 --> 00:03:12,704
It refuses zero, a negative number, a wrong unit, a minimum match confidence below the threshold, and a dormancy period that is too long.

39
00:03:12,754 --> 00:03:14,864
A typo cannot become a policy.

40
00:03:14,914 --> 00:03:19,280
The panel shows one more thing: which values are still the product default.

41
00:03:19,330 --> 00:03:25,568
So the bank can see, on any day, which decisions it made itself, and which it took from the product.

42
00:03:25,618 --> 00:03:27,440
Back to the missing line.

43
00:03:27,490 --> 00:03:32,192
The setup stopped, the team added the value, and version zero was complete.

44
00:03:32,242 --> 00:03:35,720
Malika's change became version one, with two names on it.

45
00:03:35,770 --> 00:03:39,056
Every value has a source, a version and an owner.

46
00:03:39,106 --> 00:03:44,192
That is why this course says the configured time or the configured limit, and never a number.

47
00:03:44,242 --> 00:03:46,064
The number belongs to the bank.

48
00:03:46,114 --> 00:03:48,800
To recap: there is no default in the code.

49
00:03:48,850 --> 00:03:53,912
The defaults and the texts come from the OpenTofu configuration, as version zero.

50
00:03:53,962 --> 00:03:56,192
A missing value stops the setup.

51
00:03:56,242 --> 00:04:01,040
Policies change in the panel with maker-checker, and installation keys do not.

52
00:04:01,090 --> 00:04:08,792
Some of those values are timeouts: how long heeyID waits for MyID, TAF or the SMS service.

53
00:04:08,842 --> 00:04:11,456
So what happens when one of them is slow, or down?

54
00:04:11,506 --> 00:04:13,256
Next: adapters.
